at they are non-blocking which means  you can run them continuously  and scale around that which is just  fantastic it's one of the reasons why  node.js can scale so fast and the eighth  means that the apis can return data send  data after it's been called and really  increase the efficiency of events as  they are triggered within node.js  so how do you implement async and  node.js and so the example that we have  on the left hand side allows us to  trigger async and it's using the async  function and as you can see in this  example  it's actually something that can be done  very quickly in this example we're  actually  making a command to logo host and app  that's running on port 3000 and then  depending on what we get if there's an  error message and then we'll have error  message go to the console log which is  the terminal window and if  we get a response to an application  then we'll actually get a positive  response which would be fetched response  so  again very quickly and easy that you use  the async function  to be able to build out and you to get  your  asynchronous code  so what is the purpose of module exports  so  a module export is a way to encapsulate  your the entire code for a solution in a  single package in a single file and then  you can then share that with other  developers so you're all working from  the same environment  so what is a callback function in  node.js a callback function is  essentially that once you run and you've  triggered an event and the response has  been given  you want to be able to then have a  callback to validate that the task is  complete  all right let's move on to the final set  of questions which is advanced node.js  questions  so what is repple in node.js so repl  stands for read eval print and loop and  this is a reference to the  unix and linux background that node.js  has  so raffle will form the following tasks  the read will be actually  passing the javascript you'll read that  and store it in memory eval  takes and evaluates the data structure  print will then print the results often  on the console window or onto a web page  and then loop um loops the above command  until the user presses control c twice  what is the control flow function so the  control flow function is a piece of code  that runs between several of the  asynchronous functions  and that's it it actually controls how  those functions execute  so how control flow controls the  function calls and it does it through  four ways one is it controls the order  of execution it collects the data it  limits the concurrency and then it also  calls the next step in a program again  this is a management tool for how your  functions are called within node.js  so what is the difference between fork  and spawn methods in node.js  so the essential difference between the  two of them is that if you use the fork  method it will actually trigger and  create a new instance of the v8 engine  the engine running node.js  and will then run the application in  that new instance  with spawn  it's somewhat similar it doesn't  actually generate a new v8 instance so  it's actually creating a new process but  it's within the same v8 instance as the  previous application that it was spawned  from  explain to me what is the use of buffer  class in node.js  so with the buffer class what you're  looking at doing is you're actually  controlling the raw data of a set of  arrays and integers and the memory  allocation outside of the v8 heap buffer  class is used because pure javascript is  not compatible with binary data  what is piping in node.js  so  piping is the ability to connect the  output of one stream to another stream  that could be a data stream it could be  any stream that you're working with  but it does normally get used to pass  data from one stream to another  so what are some of the flags used in  the rewrite operation files  so r dash is used for read w's for  opening a file for writing  a dash is opening a file for appending  so the collection that you have here you  don't have to really memorize all of  them but to have a few of them in the  back of your mind is always a good  reference point  so how would you open a file using  node.js  so  the syntax you would use would be  the  fs.open and that would then open up a  file  explain to me what callback hell is uh  so callback hell also known as the  pyramid of doom is when you have too  many  nested unreadable and unmanageable  callbacks intensively cycle within each  other and they're constantly reading to  each other and they're not able to  resolve the solution  and  this is a you know it's a big problem  and this is what why it's called  callback hell because you're not able to  finalize the the app you're working on  what is a reactive pattern in node.js  and so a reactor pattern is a concept of  non-blocking i o operations  can you explain to me the test pyramid  for node.js so the test pyramid and this  is standard for testing uh for all  devops is really split into three key  areas you have unit tests integration  tests and then end-to-end or e2e tests  the unit tests are tests that you  actually do in your raw code that you're  working on and the goal there is to be  able to validate that the code you're  writing is consistently working  integration allows you to um have tests  that integrate your raw code that you're  working with in your application with  other areas of an application and then  end to end is the whole system the whole  interface as it were  for your solution  describe the exit codes of node.js  so annexa code is a specific code that  identifies the end of a specific process  and examples of exit codes are  unused uncaught fatal exception  fatal error  internal javascript evaluation area  these are all good examples of exit  codes  so what is  a middleware can you explain the concept  of middleware in nodejs  so  with middleware it is the ability to be  able to connect to a system and get data  from it  